NEXTVI PATCHES
==============
Nextvi features a robust, context-aware and customizable patching system.
The patch2vi support program facilitates script transformations.

Apply one: ./name.sh
Apply a stack: ./lsp.sh visual.sh splits.sh
Each script applies itself, adds its name to $P2VI_PATCH and hands the
rest of the chain on, which lets a patch carrying a compat block for
another see it.
If the scripts did not run as a chain, assert the applied set:
P2VI_PATCH="lsp.sh visual.sh" ./splits.sh
Every line below is one full chain, in an order that applies.
Patches not listed are unconstrained. Combinations are generated
by compat_order in patch2vi.sh.

./lsp.sh visual.sh arrowkeys_normal.sh
./grep.sh lsp.sh visual.sh splits.sh cxref.sh
./lsp.sh rstr.sh visual.sh splits.sh incsearch.sh detect_indent.sh
./lsp.sh rstr.sh visual.sh splits.sh incsearch.sh
./lsp.sh visual.sh splits.sh threaded_redraw.sh linewrap_v2.sh
./lsp.sh visual.sh splits.sh threaded_redraw.sh linewrap_v2.sh mouse_normal.sh
./lsp.sh visual.sh splits.sh
./lsp.sh rstr.sh visual.sh splits.sh arrowkeys_insert.sh incsearch.sh detect_indent.sh tab_complete.sh
./rstr.sh threaded_search.sh
./lsp.sh visual.sh

patch2vi.sh
--------------
Maintainer utils.

64bit.sh
--------------
Converts the entire codebase to 64 bit integers instead of 32 bit.
This enables Nextvi to read files and buffers up to 9,223,372 terabytes.
In addition, this may improve performance on 64 bit CPUs due to
matching pointer <---> integer alignment and possibly more compiler
optimizations.
Run this after you've applied the patches.

gen-widechars.sh
--------------
A script I use to update zwchars and dwchars in uc.c

arrowkeys_normal.sh
--------------
Adds arrow key support in normal mode.

arrowkeys_insert.sh
--------------
Adds arrow key support in insert and command modes. In command
mode, Up/Down arrows select lines from the history buffer.

threaded_search.sh
--------------
Multithreaded version of lbuf_search(). Adjust the number of threads in use
and buffer stepping in lbuf.c. Ensure that your system supports POSIX threads,
aka libpthread.

async.sh
--------------
Adds 2 new ex commands "as" and "as!". An ex command in the command chain
after "as" runs on a separate thread. "as!" is a join command that waits
for all threads to finish. POSIX thread support is required. The patch is
intended for expert users because races are possible depending on the
commands used.
Example: run the :fd command without blocking the editor
:as:fd

threaded_redraw.sh
--------------
Moves the main redraw onto a separate thread. This frees up the input thread
and reduces the input latency. No editor state is ever shared because the
frame is joined before the next command runs. POSIX thread support is required.

hl_part.sh
--------------
Allows syntax highlighting to continue past the terminal dimensions up to
a certain limit. To customize the depth, adjust the LEDFORW and LEDBACK
definitions in led.c.
Alters the meaning of the <^> and <$> assertions in syntax highlighting
regex, as the line is considered to start/end beyond the visible columns.
Works in the reverse direction and with reordered text.

hl_naive.sh
--------------
Allow syntax highlight to continue past the terminal dimensions unlimited.
Performance will degrade on very long lines.

blockhl_cache.sh
--------------
Cache multiline syntax highlighting blocks. Falls outside the scope
of core Nextvi as it violates the immediate mode rendering principle and
is more complex.

debug.sh
--------------
Frees reachable memory upon termination.
Use for debugging.
Note: in some features leaks won't be freed - for example hlw (which is not
enabled by default). Also initializes sdense in regex.c so that valgrind
stops freaking out.

grep.sh
--------------
Adds a special temporary buffer /grep/ much like the default /hist/ or /fm/.
When the new ^x keybind is executed, vi performs fs_search on every file and
stores the results in the /grep/ buffer. Finally, the user is switched to
/grep/. Line information is stored and displayed with new syntax highlighting
rules. The searched keyword is also highlighted. Use the familiar keybind
^i or TAB on the wanted line to immediately open the file and go to the
line specified. To quickly open /grep/ use the new keybind: vx
The format of the stored lines is specifically designed to mirror the output
of grep -r -n "str" .
The feature can be substituted with a pipe command in case of a higher
performing grep implementation or loading of cached results from a script.

cxref.sh
--------------
Adds a special temporary buffer /xref/ and the keybind gx, for C only.
gx collects the definition of the symbol under the cursor and, recursively,
the definitions of everything it calls, then switches to /xref/. A count
limits how deep it goes: 1gx is the definition alone, 2gx adds what it
calls, no count means unlimited. To quickly open /xref/ use the new keybind:
vf
Every definition gets a header line in grep -n style, followed by its
code verbatim, highlighted as C. TAB on a header opens that file at that
line, which is where you edit: /xref/ itself is only for reading.
Definitions are looked up in the C files :fd lists.

rstr.sh
--------------
Adaptation from upstream Neatvi, where simple operations are optimized by
running a specialized search routine instead of the full regex engine. rstr
can yield up to 30% faster search on non-regex patterns. Not included in
base Nextvi due to the underlying complexity.

incsearch.sh
--------------
Search as you go in the / and ? prompts. Every typed key redraws the screen
with the matches of the keyword highlighted and the cursor moved to the first
match, scrolling the buffer when the match is off screen. Enter keeps
that position and searches normally, leaving counts and messages
unchanged. An
interrupt, or an erase key on an already empty keyword, restores the view,
the previous keyword and the search direction. While in the prompt TAB moves
to the next match and ^_ moves in reverse.

stdin_pipe.sh
--------------
Lets you use vi like a pager (less), except you need to specify - as a
command line argument (just like in vim).
Example:
$ dmesg | vi -

ec_closebuf.sh
--------------
Allows the user to close a buffer using the new ex command "cx".
Specify a number as an argument to select which buffer to close; with no
argument, the current buffer is closed.
When a buffer is closed, all buffers above it are shifted and renumbered.
A range can be supplied like so: :cx0-5
This closes buffers 0 to 5 inclusive.
Omitting a parameter of the range assumes the current buffer index for
that parameter (the range is then not inclusive on that side).
Example: :cx-5 or :cx5-

filetype_shebang.sh
--------------
Detect and set the corresponding filetype based on the shebang
or specific data in the file. Currently supports only shell
and python.

kbd_layout.sh
--------------
Changes hjkl to work well with other keyboard layouts.
In this example hjkl is changed to yneo (workman keyboard layout)
Note: this patch can be used as a base for remapping any base keybinds.

quickexit.sh
--------------
Added kj sequence to quickly exit insert mode.
There is one caveat: if input comes too fast, for example a paste from the
terminal, and the sequence has "kj" in it, insert mode is cancelled
mid-way and the paste is wrong. Luckily this almost never happens, as such
letter combinations are extremely rare.
The delay time (in ms) can be set via :qe N or disabled by setting N to 0.

hl_mark.sh
-------------
Adds a new ex option "hlm" which highlights lbuf marks. Each mark has its
own color, up to 16; higher marks may duplicate the colors.
Lazy redraw, use <^L> if needed.

c_option.sh
-------------
Adds the -c command line option. This can be used to pass in ex commands instead
of EXINIT.

exrc.sh
-------------
Adds support for reading the .exrc file from the XDG, .config or home directories.
This can be used to pass in ex commands instead of EXINIT.

readonly.sh
-------------
Adds a new ex option "ro" and the read-only -R command line option

remove_vla_cliterals.sh
--------------
Removes variable length arrays and compound literals for portability reasons.

undojump.sh
--------------
Adds a new keybind ^o which allows jumping through the undo marks
without actually undoing.

undo_branches.sh
--------------
Adds a basic multi-level undo implementation. New ex command "up"
prints divergent changes. Each path has an index; switch to it
with "up" and a number as an argument. Example: :up1
The current undo branch is stored as the last index before swapping.

alternate-w-behaviour.sh
--------------
Implements more vim-like d/c/yw

ac_context.sh
--------------
Improvements to autocomplete: the word completion now also uses
text that prefixes the insert when it makes sense. This makes it
possible to autocomplete in more situations.

tab_complete.sh
--------------
Adds path completion to the ex prompt, bound to TAB. The word before
the cursor is completed as a path, relative to the working directory
or to the directory that the word itself names. A single match is
filled in, with a trailing </> when it is a directory. Several matches
fill in their common prefix, and once nothing is left to fill in, they
are listed. With "tc" set to 1 the prompt never scrolls: the list is
drawn next to the prompt and the cursor stays where it was. With "tc"
set to 2 the screen is cleared and the list is drawn below the prompt
instead, wrapped at word boundaries; TAB then pages it and any other
key leaves it. New keybind ^_ completes like "tc" set to 2 whatever
"tc" is, keeping the full listing always one key away.
When nothing matches, TAB inserts a literal tab.
Adds a new ex option "tc" to control the completion, on by default.
Example: turn it off, TAB is then always a literal tab
:tc 0

linewrap_v1.sh
--------------
Soft line wrap implementation using a buffer only approach.
Adds a new :lw command which can be used to specify the maximum column
at which soft linewrap lines break. Given no argument the column is
elastic: it tracks the terminal width and the buffer is re-wrapped
on every resize, as soon as it holds no unsaved changes.
Adds a new ex option "hllw" to highlight linewrapped boundaries.
Trades off flexibility for simplicity. Cannot change linewrapping
while the buffer is modified. Different edit semantics: visual edits
are line based.

linewrap_v2.sh
--------------
Soft line wrap implementation using a render only approach.
Adds a new :lw command which can be used to specify the maximum column
at which soft linewrap lines break. Given no argument it toggles an
elastic column, which follows the terminal across resizes, or the
window it is drawn in with splits.sh.
Adds a new ex option "hllw" to highlight linewrapped boundaries.
No limitation from v1. Default edit semantics. Visual edits are
geometry based.

ex-scripting.sh
--------------
Adds new ex commands "sr" and "sx".
NEXTVI_FT - current buffer filetype
NEXTVI_ROW - current line number
NEXTVI_OFF - current byte offset
NEXTVI_COFF - current character offset
NEXTVI_LINE - current line
NEXTVI_WORD - current word
"sr" runs a command with the environment variables above
"sx" runs a command with the environment variables above and
executes its stdout as ex commands

chunked_read_write.sh
--------------
Reduces the upper memory requirement for reading files. By default vi
reads the whole file into the buffer and then splits it into lines; with
this patch the file is read in 4k chunks and split into lines as it
loads. This helps a lot when reading binary files: the whole file no
longer has to be loaded into memory just to find out that it should
be truncated.
In addition, it reduces the number of write syscalls by using chunks
when writing a file.

visual.sh
--------------
Adds character-wise (gv), line-wise (gV), and block (gb) visual selection
modes. Move the cursor to extend the selection; the selected region is
highlighted with inverted colors. Press the same key again to exit
that mode; ESC also cancels the selection.
The current mode (-- VISUAL --, -- VISUAL LINE --, -- VISUAL BLOCK --)
appears in the ^g status bar; when the permanent status bar is enabled
it shows continuously alongside the file information.
Supported operators applied to the selection:
  y  yank          d  delete         c/C/S  change
  >  indent        <  dedent         !  pipe through shell command
  ~  toggle case   u  lowercase      U  uppercase
  :  enter ex prompt with a scoped range
Block mode (gb) also supports:
  I  insert at the left edge of the block on every selected line;
     lines shorter than the left edge are skipped
  A  append after the right edge of the block on every selected line;
     for lines shorter than the right edge, text is appended at EOL

splits.sh
--------------
Implements "sp" and "vs" ex commands for horizontal and vertical splits.
Adds the vc and vd normal commands to switch and close views. A view can
also be closed with "q".
The new ex command "eq" can be used to balance the vertical and horizontal
split space in the nearest dimension.

alt_sections.sh
--------------
Implements section keybindings closer to standard vi(1):
[#])          Move to next sentence boundary down # times
[#](          Move to next sentence boundary up # times
[#]]          Move to next <{> section down # times
[#][          Move to next <{> section up # times
[#]}          Move to next <Newline> section down # times
[#]{          Move to next <Newline> section up # times

key_remap_cmds.sh
--------------
Adds "im", "im!", "nm" and "nm!" ex commands to dynamically remap keybinds
in normal and insert modes.
Remapping a to hello in insert mode
:im ahello
Remapping a to i in normal mode
:nm ai

modal.sh
--------------
Integrates modal programming language interpreter.
https://wiki.xxiivv.com/site/modal.html
The new ex command [vrange]md[script] evaluates the contents of the
current buffer and the argument as a modal script.
Adds a new phase, ($ ...), to execute ex command chains
from within the modal script.

lsp.sh
--------------
LSP support for Nextvi.
Adds a new ex command "lsp" to start the LSP server, for example:
:lsp c clangd
Check that it is running:
:lsp
Once the server is up, new normal keybinds can be used:
gK - show hover documentation for the symbol at the cursor
gd - goto definition of the symbol at the cursor
Also supports on-the-fly hinting.

sse.sh
--------------
SIMD optimizations for Nextvi.

mouse_normal.sh
--------------
Adds mouse support in normal mode, with a new ex option "ms" to toggle it.
Clicking positions the cursor and the mouse wheel scrolls using ^Y or ^E.
Terminal mouse selections can still be made while holding Shift on most
terminal emulators.

detect_indent.sh
--------------
Scans lines to guess the indentation format.
Adds 3 new ex options:
"et" - expandtab. Tabs are replaced with spaces.
"sw" - space width. How many literal spaces are injected for a tab.
"idt" - indent detect. Enables indent guessing and sets the line
limit (default 500).

mhash.sh
--------------
A hash map implementation for marks which aims to take O(n) mark
search down to ~O(1). An exercise. Unjustified complexity for
regular use.

writeall.sh
--------------
Adds the "wa", "wa!", "wqa", "wqa!", "xa" and "xa!" ex commands to write
all buffers to the filesystem. q commands always write, while x commands
only write modified buffers.

ins_cursor.sh
--------------
Changes cursor to a thin vertical bar when in insert mode.
This is a good indicator for insert mode.

cbas.sh
--------------
Adds syntax highlighting for Coders Basilisk
https://codeberg.org/gek/coders_basilisk/src/branch/main

highlight_cmd.sh
--------------
Adds a new ex command "hi". This command can be used to create
additional syntax highlighting rules on demand. The command prefix
specifies comma separated attributes, one per regex group, each a
bitmask conf.c can express. Figuring out the right value requires
some effort and reading vi.h helps.
Example: highlight int as keyword (RE color)
:1hi int
Example: highlight multiple different keywords
:0,1,5,3hi \<(int)|(void)|(if)\>
Example: disable rules
:hi
Example: highlight text inside parens
:5hi \(.*?\)

My current EXINIT setup:
--------------
How it works:
|sc!||1sc | - disable special characters
97reg - put macro into a register
ish - enable interactive shell
inc (^(?:(?!^\.git).)+[^/]+$) always excludes the .git folder from :fd
pac - print autocomplete options
|sc|? - default special characters; ? is a nop cycle required for
separator reset. Scripts can append more commands to EXINIT.

Macros:
@q = creates a structure like this:
{

}
@w = creates a structure like this:
whatever {
	whatever
	a
}
@e = brings :e !p $c into the ex prompt
Note: p is an alias for printf. $c[0-9] is an env var containing some path.
@a = creates a comment like this:
/* whatever */
@s = from the cursor creates a comment like this:
/* whatever */
@d = uncomment /* */ comment
@x = indent % region left
@f = brings :cd !p $c into the ex prompt
Note: p is an alias for printf. $c[0-9] is an env var containing some path.
@l = brings the !cp % %_|w|!git diff --no-index %_ % macro into the ex prompt.
Use it to create patch and diff files fast. It relies on
you not saving the file prior to running the macro.
@z = control character mapping placeholders
@t = works with the ./tags file generated by ctags
and goes to the definition matching the current regex keyword
@r = goto definition while in the ./tags file

export EXINIT="$(printf '%b' '|sc!||1sc |:108reg :!cp % %_:w:!git diff --no-index %_ %113reg o{\n}kA\n	115reg i/*A*/119reg A {jo}koa120reg >%<<$%<<122reg :ph1 1 2 1^A:ph2 2 2 1^B:ph3 3 2 1^C:ph4 4 2 1^D:ph5 5 2 1^E:ph6 6 2 1^F:ph7 7 2 1^G:ph8 8 2 1^H:ph9 9 2 1^I:ph10 10 2 1^J:ph11 11 2 1^K:ph12 12 2 1^L:ph13 13 2 1^M:ph14 14 2 1^N:ph15 15 2 1^O:ph16 16 2 1^P:ph17 17 2 1^Q:ph18 18 2 1^R:ph19 19 2 1^S:ph20 20 2 1^T:ph21 21 2 1^U:ph22 22 2 1^V:ph23 23 2 1^W:ph24 24 2 1^X:ph25 25 2 1^Y:ph26 26 2 1^Z:ph27 27 2 1^[:ph28 28 2 1^\\\\:ph29 29 2 1^]:ph30 30 2 1^^:ph31 31 2 1^_102reg :cd !p $c101reg :e !p $c97reg I/*A*/100reg /\/\*\n2x/\*\/\n2x116reg :51reg %@47:e ./tags:1;0:;>unescaped>:??!\%s/[\\\\](.)/\\1/g\:1i unescaped:ic:%f>^%@51	:??!p "%@51" not found in tags\:ic\:e %#\:re %@51\:1q:;>	>+1;#>	>ya49:;>	/\^>+3;#>\$/;">re:??!;>	[0-9]+>+1;#>;>ya50\:??\!p bad format\\\\\:ic\\\\\:e %#\\\\\:re %@51\\\\\:2q\:e %@49\:?\%@50\:re %@51\:ic\:1q:e %@49:1:%f>:re %@51:ic114reg :51reg %@47:;>	>+1;#>	>ya49:;>	/\^>+3;#>\$/;">re:??!;>	[0-9]+>+1;#>;>ya50\:??\!p bad format\\\\\:re %@51\\\\\:2q\:e %@49\:?\%@50\:re %@51\:1q:e %@49:1:ic:%f>:re %@51:icinc (^(?:(?!^\.git).)+[^/]+$)pacish|sc|?')"
